WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.402
-I haven't had a chance to see
you after "Avengers" came out.

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:06.139
But it went on to make
like $2.9 billion.

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:09.041
-Yeah, it made a few bucks.
-Yeah, it did.

00:00:09.041 --> 00:00:11.944
It's like the biggest grossing
movie in the history of film.

00:00:11.944 --> 00:00:13.546
-Right, yeah.
-Not too shabby, buddy.

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:15.114
Congrats on that.
-Yeah, not too shabby.

00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:16.615
I'll take it. I'll take it.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:16.615 --> 00:00:18.618
And everyone's probably already
asked you all these questions,

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:19.652
so I won't --

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:20.853
-Lay them on me.
What do you got?

00:00:20.853 --> 00:00:22.254
-Well, I mean,
was it sentimental?

00:00:22.254 --> 00:00:24.858
Did you -- Were you sad when you
had to do the end -- last scene?

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:26.292
And --
-Yeah, it was very emotional.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:27.893
I mean, it's
surprisingly emotional.

00:00:27.893 --> 00:00:29.929
These movies are
a huge part of your life.

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:31.597
And so, when they
come to an end,

00:00:31.597 --> 00:00:33.466
it really -- It has an impact.

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:35.501
-Were you wearing, like,
dots on your face

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:37.570
or CGI, like, ping-pong balls
on your face?

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.405
-No, luckily, I don't
have to do that nonsense.

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:41.607
But it still -- It still --

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:43.643
You know, it still
had an effect.

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:45.244
-Well, yeah, I mean,
but you're doing the thing.

00:00:45.244 --> 00:00:47.980
But in the movie when you see
it, I mean you have the --

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.049
I can't spoil it
at this point, right?

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:53.385
-No, no, yeah. This is so nice
'cause this is after --

00:00:53.385 --> 00:00:55.321
-People have seen it.
-We can say anything we want.

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:56.355
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:56.355 --> 00:00:58.157
But you're old.
-Yeah.

00:00:58.157 --> 00:01:01.126
-They did such an amazing job
making you old.

00:01:01.126 --> 00:01:02.695
Did that freak you out?
-They did a good job.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:05.664
Well, they did -- They did --
A lot of it was prosthetic.

00:01:05.664 --> 00:01:07.967
They left my mouth
without the prosthetic

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:09.569
so they could add that CGI.

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:12.472
I guess that's actually still --
still hard to do.

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:14.040
But they left my mouth the same,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.877
but even just having the whole
face, you know, you kind of --

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:19.345
It's like looking
at your grandfather.

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:20.847
-Yeah.
-It's a strange feeling.

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:22.815
-Well, the good news is
you're a good-looking old man.

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:24.483
[ Laughter ]
You're going to age very well.

00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:25.784
-That's what I heard,
yeah, yeah.

00:01:25.784 --> 00:01:27.253
-Did Anthony Mackie --

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:30.022
I know all the scripts and all
the scenes are very secretive.

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:32.024
And did he know that you were
going to give him the shield?

00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:34.160
-You know, there's actually
a really great story with that.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.430
The secret -- The scripts
are so lock and key,

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.166
but they had given me the scene

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:42.368
where I pass the shield
to Mackie.

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:44.303
Oh, it's so nice
to be able to say that.

00:01:44.303 --> 00:01:45.838
You know, it doesn't
have to be a secret.

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:46.839
-It's like therapy.

00:01:46.839 --> 00:01:48.508
I want you to lay
down on the couch

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:51.577
and just talk about
the Marvel secrets, yeah.

00:01:51.577 --> 00:01:54.881
-But -- But -- I did know that
Mackie was getting the shield,

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:57.516
and while we -- This is actually
a really nice story.

00:01:57.516 --> 00:01:59.618
While we were filming
in Atlanta,

00:01:59.618 --> 00:02:01.521
you know, I had already
read the scene.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:04.156
I had a few people over to watch
a football game or something,

00:02:04.156 --> 00:02:06.058
and Mackie was the first one
to show up.

00:02:06.058 --> 00:02:09.429
I didn't know he didn't know
what was going to happen.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:10.797
And he showed up first,
and I said,

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:13.299
"Hey, man, you know,
isn't that scene fantastic?"

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:15.935
And he said, "What scene?"

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:19.305
And I said, "The -- The scene
where I give you the shield."

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.407
And he said,
"You're giving me the shield?"

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:22.909
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:26.245
And I said, "Oh, no. Oh, no."

00:02:26.245 --> 00:02:29.115
Well, yeah." And so I ran
to my room, and I got the scene,

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:33.720
and I gave it to him, and I got
to watch him read the scene

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.722
for the first time
where he's getting the shield.

00:02:35.722 --> 00:02:38.391
And he was so happy, and, you
know, you immediately feel like,

00:02:38.391 --> 00:02:41.761
man, maybe I -- Maybe I robbed
this moment from --

00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:44.364
Maybe Kevin Feige deserved this.

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:46.866
But it was so nice to share it
with him.

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:50.336
He was so happy and, you know,
so deserving, and it was just --

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:51.503
-It was a great moment.

00:02:51.503 --> 00:02:53.172
-It was a nice way
to kind of have,

00:02:53.172 --> 00:02:56.075
just between us,
the transition of characters.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:57.209
It was great.

00:02:57.209 --> 00:02:58.811
-I got to see it
in a theater full of people,

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:01.881
and one of the biggest
parts of the movie

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:05.117
that got a big reaction is
when you pick up Thor's hammer.

00:03:05.117 --> 00:03:06.753
-Yeah.
-That was a big moment.

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:08.554
Yeah, I mean --
Everyone went nuts.

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:09.956
Like, "What?!

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:11.991
I knew he could do it!"
Like, yelling.

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:14.293
"I knew he always --"
Was that --

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:15.662
-I don't think
Hemsworth liked it.

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:16.729
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.664
-"Hey, there's only one Thor,"
yeah.

00:03:18.664 --> 00:03:21.234
What would feel better, picking
up the shield for the first time

00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:23.869
or Thor's hammer
for the first time?

00:03:23.869 --> 00:03:25.537
-Hammer.
-Really? [ Laughs ]

00:03:25.537 --> 00:03:28.007
-Yeah. Well, you know,

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:30.576
the shield was destined
for the character.

00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:33.045
The hammer was kind of this,
you know,

00:03:33.045 --> 00:03:35.615
period at the end of a sentence
in terms of his worth,

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:37.016
and, yeah, it was nice.

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:39.819
And the Russos really know
how to build those moments up.

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:41.454
-They were great.
-I'll say the hammer.

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:42.655
-They did such a great job
with that.

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:44.256
Everyone just knocked it
out of the park.

00:03:44.256 --> 00:03:46.226
Let's talk about your new film,
"Knives Out."

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:47.827
-Yeah.
-A totally different character

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.328
than Captain America.
-Yeah.

00:03:49.328 --> 00:03:53.065
Well, we finished
the last scenes of "Endgame,"

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:54.934
and I went into "Knives Out"
only a week later.

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:57.303
So it really was a --
It was a real 180...

00:03:57.303 --> 00:03:58.704
-Wow.
-...in terms of character.

00:03:58.704 --> 00:04:00.506
-Can you explain what the movie
is about to everybody?

00:04:00.506 --> 00:04:03.075
-I can try.
It's kind of a murder mystery

00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:06.479
in the vein of Agatha Christie.

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:08.614
Everyone is vile.
Everyone is awful.

00:04:08.614 --> 00:04:09.983
Everyone's a suspect.

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:12.385
But those are the movies I love.

00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:14.854
You know, those -- Those are
the movies -- It's like "Clue."

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:16.722
-Yeah. Oh, yeah. Yeah.
-These are the movies --

00:04:16.722 --> 00:04:18.191
-With a great cast.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:21.427
-Daniel Craig is in it.
Michael Shannon.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:22.929
-So many -- Jamie Lee Curtis.

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:24.197
-Jamie Lee Curtis.

00:04:24.197 --> 00:04:25.698
Don Johnson.
-Don Johnson.

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:27.266
-This guy -- I love that.
-Toni Collette?

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:28.600
Come on.
-Oh, Toni Collette.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:30.270
I mean, you stop right there.
-She can do no wrong.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.470
-Yeah, exactly.
-And that's just --

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.472
I mean, across the board,
everyone was --

00:04:33.472 --> 00:04:34.874
-You don't know
who's telling the truth

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:36.275
and who's lying
the whole way through,

00:04:36.275 --> 00:04:37.977
which is what you want
a good mystery to be like.

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:39.745
Are you a good liar
in real life?

00:04:39.745 --> 00:04:42.882
-No, I'm a terrible liar,
which is so weird,

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:45.150
because I act for a living.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.020
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:48.888
-But, you know,
you when have lines

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:51.357
that you're supposed to say
in the context of filming,

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:52.691
it's great.

00:04:52.691 --> 00:04:56.395
When it's actually real-life
lying, I feel like I --

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:59.198
I have every tell
you could possibly have.

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:00.867
I get red. I sweat.

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:03.002
My eyes gaze.
I'm touching my face.

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:04.737
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:06.038
-You can't --
You don't have a poker face.

00:05:06.038 --> 00:05:07.372
-I'm horrible at poker,

00:05:07.372 --> 00:05:09.075
which is such a shame,
'cause I love the game,

00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:11.844
but the second the cards
are dealt, I'm like --

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:14.246
[ Giggling ]

00:05:14.246 --> 00:05:17.249
-"Ah, I got an ace!
Oh, no, I didn't."

00:05:17.249 --> 00:05:18.451
[ Both babbling ]

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:20.386
Like, we can't play
with you anymore, dude.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:23.055
-Out the gate. It'd be easier
if I just give you my money,

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:24.891
and I'm gonna go
have a drink at the bar.

